Size-control of particulate materials might be an effective strategy for biomaterials majorization [10, 20, 21]. A number of micron-sized particulate materials gained the advantage of being synthesized simply and straightforwardly, yet their applications had encountered many limitations while playing a precise role in tiny cells or smaller organelles [5, 20, 22]. In order to pursue further development, many researchers have modified the original micron-scale materials into nanoscale materials, thus opened the door to broader applications. Due to their different particle sizes, the modified materials displayed respectively unique chemical and/or physical properties that could differ quite extensively from the properties of the corresponding original materials, such as structure [23], bioavailability [24], biodegradability and in vivo elimination [25], etc. The modification of silica, for example, had turned micron-sized silica particles to nano-sized mesoporous silica particles (NSNP), which was a very significant improvement
process. Following this change in particle size, the original unremarkable micron particles turned into a star of biological materials with its applications expanded greatly to, for instance, drug delivery, fluorescence tracking and composite modification [22, 26]. A similar and classic beneficiary was graphene oxide (GO), with its nanoscale particles recognized as one of the universal carriers for anticancer or other targeted drugs delivery systems, while micron-sized graphene oxides showed powerlessness [27]. Similarly, we found that the particle size diversity of MOFs, without changing their types or structures, might bring noticeable improvement to their biological functions. For instance, the transformation of micron-sized ZIF-8 crystals into its nanoparticles could not only alleviate the biotoxicity but also endow ZIF-8 with satisfying ability to promote osteogenic differentiation as a surface coating on porous titanium [16]. Additionally, Tamames et al. stated in their study that nanosized Fe-MOF showed better biosafety [28]. Therefore, this concise and predictable improvement of particle size might reveal a new strategy for the development of MOFs' biological potential.
MOF74 is a family of classic MOF composed of a series of metal ions clusters and bishydroxydicarboxylic acid as their linkers. Among these, Mg-MOF74 containing \({\mathrm{Mg}}^{2+}\) core is an important member [29]. Magnesium is an essential ion to the human body and plays a critical role in the life process, such as its participation in enzymatic reactions [30], antioxidation and anti-apoptosis [31], as well as its involvement in osteogenesis [32] and angiogenesis [32, 33]. Shen and his team deposited the magnesium/mac-metal organic framework \((\mathrm{Mg}/\) Zn-MOF74) on a titanium surface to construct a stable micron-sized MOF74 modified sample. The modified implants exhibited antibacterial and anti-inflammatory properties with new bone formation improved [34]. Mg-MOF74 was also constructed with strontium-substituted hydroxyapatite (Sr-HA) on titanium surfaces to ameliorate the local injury treatment [35]. However, limited by its biological availability and incomplete biological effect, the independent application of Mg-MOF74 in biomaterials was still rare. But fortunately, the appearance of nano-scale Mg-MOF74 [36] was able to bring us inspiration. It still remained unknown how the biological properties of nanoscale Mg-MOF74 would change in comparison with its micron-sized form, and could the modification of particle size become the key to exploiting the application of Mg-MOF74 or other MOFs?
Although progress had been made regarding the synthesis and medical application of multiple nano-MOFs, no systematic evaluation of biosafety and potential biological effects had been done in contrast with their micron-scale particles, which was especially important for future biomedical applications of these materials. Herein, in combination with the biological status of magnesium, we conducted a detailed research to investigate and compare both in vitro and in vivo biosafety of the micron- and nano-scale Mg-MOF74 \(\mathrm{m/n-Mg-MOF74})\) particles. Firstly, micron- and nano-scale Mg-MOF74 were synthesized and characterized. Then in vitro and in vivo cytocompatibilities contrast under suitable dosage were analyzed. Based on the previous results, the biological effects of MgMOF74 (including osteogenesis and angiogenesis) were explored to assess the optimal effects of particle size changes on biological potential. The outcomes revealed that such alteration was specifically able to broaden the biological applications of Mg-MOF74 including the optimization of in vitro and in vivo biocompatibility, the extension of its biosafety range and the efficiency of utilization in bone regeneration and angiogenesis process. This study not only demonstrated that particle size
modification was a viable means to optimize the biological properties of Mg-MOF74 and provided a strategic springboard for the improvement of the MOF74 family, but also created a new horizon for the research of biological functionalization of MOFs.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Materials
\(\mathrm{Mg}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},\) 2,5-dihydroxyterephthalic acid (H4dhtp), N,N'- dimethylformamide (DMF), aqueous ammonia \(\left(\mathrm{NH}_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)\) , polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP) and ethanol were purchased from the Aladdin Biochemical Technology Co., Ltd. (Shanghai, China). HeLa cells, HUVECs were supplied by the State Key Laboratory of Oral Diseases, Sichuan University (Chengdu, China). Dulbecco's modified Eagle's medium (DMEM), \(\upalpha\) -minimum essential medium ( \(\mathbf{\dot{a}}\) -MEM), Roswell Park Memorial Institute 1640 medium (RPMI 1640), fetal bovine serum (FBS), trypsin and penicillin/streptomycin (PS) were purchased from Gibco (Gibco, Grand Island, NY, USA). Cell Counting Kit-8 assay, Annexin V-FITC/Propidium Iodide (PI) Cell Apoptosis Kit were purchased from Dojindo (Japan). Fluorescein isothiocyanate phalloidin (FITC-phalloidin) was acquired from Yeasen Biotechnology Co., Ltd. (Shanghai, China). The Alp activity kit and the nitric oxide test kit were from Jiancheng Co. (Nanjing, China). A bicinchoninic acid (BCA) protein assay kit, 4,6-diamidino-2-phenylindole (DAPI), reactive oxygen species assay kit (ROS), nicotinamide adenine deucleotide phosphate assay kit (NADPH), alkaline phosphatase coloring reagent (NBT/BCIP) and hematoxylin and eosin (H&E), were purchased from the Beyotime Institute of Biotechnology (Shanghai, China). The enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay kits (ELISA) were supplied by the CloudClone Corp. (Wuhan, China). The RNAzol reagent was acquired from TaKaRa (Dalian, China). The iScript cDNA synthesis kit and universal SYBR Green Supermix were purchased from Bio-Rad (Shanghai, China). A \(0.1\%\) Sirius Red solution, isoflurane, sodium pentobarbital, and buprenorphine were obtained from Sigma Aldrich (St. Louis, USA). Sprague-Dawley rats were provided from Experimental Animal Center, Sichuan University (Chengdu, China). All the other chemicals and reagents were of reagent grade and they were all purchased from Bioscience Regents Company (Chengdu, China).
2.2 Fabrication of \(\mathbf{m}/\mathbf{n}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{O}\mathbf{F}\mathbf{7}\mathbf{4}\)
For \(\mathrm{m-Mg-MOF74}\) , we employed a suspension including H4dhtp \(\left(1\;\mathrm{mmol}\right)\) , \(\mathrm{Mg}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) \(\left(2\mathrm{~mmol}\right)\) and PVP \(\left(100\;\mathrm{mg}\right)\) mixed with DMF \(\mathrm{[60~mL)}\) and deionized water \(\mathrm{[5~mL]}\) as the mother suspension. This suspension was prepared in a \(200~\mathrm{mL}\) Teflon-lined autoclave and heated in an oven at \(393~\mathrm{K}\) for \(^{8\mathrm{~h~}}\) and then it was cooled down in the oven naturally for another \(4\,\mathrm{~h~}\) . The resultant suspension was washed by DMF with centrifugation for 3 times \((5,000\;\mathrm{rpm},10\;\mathrm{min})\) and in sequence by ethanol with centrifugation for another 3 times \((5,000\;\mathrm{rpm},\) \(10\;\mathrm{min})\) . The obtained productions were dried under nitrogen at \(323\;\mathrm{K}\) for \(24\,\mathrm{{h}}\) . On the other hand, for n-Mg-MOF74 it was synthesized by adding \(25\%\) aqueous ammonia \(\left(\mathrm{NH}_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right.\) , \(0.5\;\mathrm{mL}\) ) to the mother suspension. The successive protocols of the fabrication were totally identical with m-Mg-MOF74.
## 2.3 Characterization of micro/nano Mg-MOF74
Powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of the obtained Mg-MOF74 crystals were analyzed using an X'Pert pro MPD diffractometer (Philips, Japan) with Cu Kα radiation (15 mA and 40 kV) at a scan rate of 2 °min-1 and the range of angles
from \(5^{\circ}-50^{\circ}\) \((2\theta=5^{\circ}-50^{\circ})\). The morphology of Mg-MOF74 was observed by a scanning electron microscopy (SEM) instrument (KYKY Technology Development Ltd.) which was operated at \(10\;\mathrm{kV}\). Energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) was analyzed by Inca (KYKY Technology Development Ltd.).
## 2.4 Cell culture
The Hela cells were cultured in RPMI 1640 medium and the human umbilical vein endothelial cells (HUVEC) were cultured in low sugar-DMEM supplemented with \(10\%\) FBS and \(1\%\) PS respectively. The femur and tibia were dissected from 2 to 3 weeks old Sprague–Dawley (SD) rat, and bone marrow was flushed out with a needle into \(\alpha\)-MEM medium with \(10\%\) FBS and \(1\%\) PS. Nonadherent cells were removed after \(48\,\mathrm{~h~}\) by washing with PBS and the fresh medium was replenished. Cells were passaged every 6 days. Bone marrow mesenchymal stem cells (BMSCs) Passages 3 or 4 (P3 or P4-BMSCs) were used for the following experiments. All these cells lived in a humidified atmosphere of \(5\%\) \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) at \(37\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\).
