## 2.18 Blood biochemistry assay
We carried out quantitatively assay on blood urea nitrogen (BUN), creatinine, lactate dehydrogenase (LDH-L), calcium, magnesium, creatine kinase (CK), alanine aminotransferase (ALT) and aspartate aminotransferase (AST) using the kits (Huili Co., Changchun, China). The activities of glutathione peroxidase (GSH-Px) and superoxide dismutase (SOD) in rats sera were determined with kits from Nanjing Jianchen Bioengineering Institute, China.
## 2.19 Histological examination
For histological evaluation, heart, liver, spleen and lung tissue specimens were immediately collected and fixed with \(10\%\) formalin in PBS after rats were sacrificed. Samples were embedded in a paraffin block, and then sliced into \(5~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) thick sections onto glass slides. Following standard protocols, H&E staining was performed. The process of calvarial was the same with other tissues, followed with H&E Masson's Trichrome, Sirius red. The Alp and CD31 fluorochrome staining kit were purchased from Affinity Company, USA. Slides were observed and captured with a microscope (OLYMPUS Co., Japan).
## 2.20 Statistical analyses
The results of the experiments were statistically analyzed using SPSS version 19.0 software. All data were presented as mean \(\pm\) standard deviation (SD). One-way ANOVA followed by Tukey's post-test was also performed to assess statistical difference using SPSS software and the statistical significance was declared when \(p<0.05\).
## 3 Result and discussion
In this study, we synthesized (Fig. 1(a)) and characterized micron-scale Mg-MOF74 and nano-scale Mg-MOF74 particles (Figs. 1(b)–1(f)). The micron/nano-scale Mg-MOF74s were simply expressed as m-Mg-MOF74 and n-Mg-MOF74, respectively. A complete in vitro and in vivo safety assessment was then completed for these two materials. In addition, we also explored the osteogenesis and angiogenesis of n-Mg-MOF74 and m-Mg-MOF74, in order to establish the foundation for the selection and construction of biomaterials in future.
## 3.1 Synthesis and characterization of Mg-MOF74
The m-Mg-MOF 74 displayed dark yellow-to-orange color after being dried under nitrogen and n-Mg-MOF74 was comparatively light-yellow crystals (Fig. S1 in the ESM). Part of the obtained final productions were then ground to powders in order that it would be convenient for successive characterization.
The morphology of the crystals was firstly observed by SEM (Fig. 1(b)) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM) (Fig. S2 in the ESM) with obvious diversity between the shape and particle sizes being easily found. Mapping patterns via SEM (Fig. S3 in the ESM) and TEM (Fig. 1(c)) were merged by three channels, carbon, oxygen and magnesium, and it revealed abundant and homogeneous magnesium distribution. Along with the results from dynamic light scattering (DLS) in Fig. 1(d), m-Mg-MOF74 exhibited a round shape with approximately \(35\%\) of the particles ranging from 3 to \(4~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\)
while the majority of n-Mg-MOF74 was rod shape crystal with much smaller size between 250 to \(350\;\mathrm{nm}\).
In order to further ensure the phase purity of the as-prepared crystals, powder XRD patterns of m-n-Mg-MOF74 (Fig. 1(e)) were gained. The graph demonstrated no discrepancy with the simulated patterns, which proved the phase purity of the productions. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) patterns of both crystals were exhibited in Fig. 1(f) and they showed remarkable peak of magnesium on the same location, which provided sufficient evidence of magnesium distribution that corresponded to the mapping patterns. EDS in Fig. S4 in the ESM was implemented along with SEM and both crystals showed conspicuous high level of magnesium in consistence with the results from mapping and XPS. Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) curves in Fig. S5 in the ESM indicated similar patterns of the two crystals that a relatively rapid weight loss from \(100\%\) to \(80\%\) was easy to be observed when the sample was heated from room temperature to \(373~\mathrm{K}\) and it generally corresponded to the vaporization of water inside crystal lattice. Then the platform of the curves lasted from 373 to approximately \(523~\mathrm{K}\), which indicated the thermal stability of both m/n-Mg-MOF74 that they could generally maintain their structures till \(523~\mathrm{K}\). Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectra (Fig. S6 in the ESM) were then measured and also showed consistency between micron and nano-scale Mg-MOF74.
## 3.2 Intracellular biocompatibility evaluation of Mg-MOF74
The cytotoxicity assay was a fundamental step to explain the cellular safety of m-Mg-MOF74 and n-Mg-MOF74, which could give us crucial information about cell survival, death, apoptosis or metabolic activities. Firstly, the initial adhesion of the Hela cells under treatment with two kinds of Mg-MOF was evaluated by SEM images (Fig. 2(a)). The adhesion function of the Hela cells appeared concentration dependence of Mg-MOF74. High concentrations of Mg-MOF74 had negative impact on cell adhesion because of the loss of adhesive structures. For example, when the concentration exceeded \(500~\upmu\mathrm{g}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\), morphologies of the cells in both the micron and nano groups were obviously changed, including decreased cell pseudopods, reduction of filament, intercellular connections and even cell deformation (m2000, n2000 in Fig. 2(a)). The same trend was also found in the results of the related statistical analysis. As was shown in Fig. S7 in the ESM, the number of unadhered cells increased under \(500~\upmu\mathrm{g}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) (n-Mg-MOF74) or \(1,000~\upmu\mathrm{g}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) (n-Mg-MOF74) treatment. Significantly, we also observed that lower concentrations of Mg-MOF could promote cell adhesion, which was similar to the results of other magnesium-based biomaterials [32].
Next, we presented more than one assay to study the viability of Hela cells, including actin filaments of cytoskeleton staining, MTT, and apoptosis double-staining assays. The morphological changes of Hela cells were recorded by cytoskeleton staining after \(12~\mathrm{h}\) treatment with two different concentrations of magnesium MOFs. In Fig. S8 in the ESM, fluorescent observations of treated cells revealed the distinct morphological changes. Hela cells could maintain their round shapes compared with control group under low doses. Nonetheless, significant shrinkage, restricted extensions and wizened nucleus (especially micron group) were gradually observed accompanied by the ascent of treated concentrations. Cell viability tests of the two Mg-MOF74 particles on Hela cells were subsequently determined by an MTT assay, and \(\mathrm{IC}_{50}\) values were calculated based on the results of the assay respectively.
After \(24\,\mathrm{~h~}\) exposure under the concentration ranging from \(50{-}2,000~\,\upmu\mathrm{g}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) , we could find dose-dependent manners similar to those of the adhesion test (Figs. 2(b) and 2(d)). Both \(\mathrm{m-Mg-MOF74}\) and \(\mathrm{n-Mg-MOF74}\) showed no significant toxicity to cells below \(200\ \upmu\mathrm{g}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) , while cytotoxicity of \(\mathrm{m-Mg-}\) MOF74 gradually increased at doses above \(500~\upmu\mathrm{g}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) . The toxicity increments of \(\mathrm{m-Mg-MOF74}\) also had the same trend with a higher dose limit \((2,000~\upmu\mathrm{g}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1})\) required to trigger.
According to the cell viability curves in Figs. 2(c) and 2(e), \(\mathrm{IC}_{50}\) of \(\mathrm{m-Mg-MOF74}\) was calculated to be \(798~\upmu\mathrm{g}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) far below that of \(\mathrm{n-Mg-MOF74}\) (more than \(2,000~\upmu\mathrm{g}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) ). In addition, it was necessary to assess the apoptotic effects of granular materials. As shown in Fig. 2(f), when cells were exposed to \(\mathrm{m/n-Mg-MOF74}\) ranging from 50 to \(500~\upmu\mathrm{g}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) , the apoptosis percentage no-significantly increased. It was worth noting that although the micron-scale group showed
more obvious ability to induce apoptosis than the nano-scale group at \(1,000\ \upmu\mathrm{g}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) (Fig. 2(g)), the difference was not significant at the highest concentration.
Oxygen free radical damage and the impediment of intracellular respiratory chains are also important factors that shall be carefully taken into account for biological applications of nano/micron-sized materials [40]. We evaluated the possible intracellular oxidative and respiratory metabolic toxicity of
\(\mathrm{m/n-Mg-MOF74}\). Reactive oxygen species assay indicated no significant oxidative toxicity of the \(\mathrm{m/m-Mg-MOF}\) generally, with only slight oxidative damage at the highest concentration (Fig. S9 in the ESM). Additionally, \(\mathrm{m/m-Mg-MOF74}\) affected nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate (NADP) levels only at \(2,000\ \upmu\mathrm{g}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) and except for this they had a wide range of safe doses (Fig. S10 in the ESM).
For further applications of potential biomaterials in medicine,
the evaluation of biocompatibility and the estimation of safety doses shall be given priorities. From the results of intracellular toxicity above and a series of published articles, it could be concluded that n-Mg-MOF74 had superior biosafety than many other nano-MOFs. The half lethal concentration of nano-Mg-MOF74 was over \(2,000~\upmu\mathrm{g}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\), which was much higher than other commonly used MOFs like nano-ZIF-8, nano-Fe(III)-MOF (MIL-88, 100, 127), etc. Different from \(Z\mathbf{n}^{2+}\) or \(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\) that has certain bactericidal or oxidative properties [41, 42], \(\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}\) is widely distributed in organism with rare oxidative toxicity [43]. On the other hand, despite the wide range of safe doses, we had observed that the cell adhesion, proliferation, apoptosis and respiration are negatively affected in a concentration-dependent manner at high concentrations (generally requiring more than \(1\;\mathrm{mg}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\)) of nano-Mg-MOF74. This situation might have close relationship to the phenomenon of "magnesium poisoning": Owing to the especially high \(\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}\) concentration in the extracellular fluid, the excessive osmotic stress tended to damage the cell membrane or even the organelle [44] and it also caused disturbance of ion action (such as excessive \(\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}\) over the simulation of \(Ca^{2+}\)) to harm cell survival [45]. Interestingly, unlike other studies on the toxicity of nanoscale materials [24], we found that n-Mg-MO74 performed better than m-Mg-MOF74 in various evaluations of toxicity. How did the safety advantage of nanoscale Mg-MOF74 occur compared to microparticles, and what were the possible reasons leading to a lower toxicity?
Therefore, to further explore the path of reduced toxicity of nanoscale Mg-MOF74, it was necessary to analyze the possible sources of toxicity of MOFs. Considering the similarity of MOFs and other nano-oxide particles, this large distinction in toxicity might be contributed to the differences in metal cores carried by MOFs and their diverse deposition time [41]. As shown in Figs. 2(h) and 2(i), the release curves showed that the \(\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}\) delivered from the two Mg-MOF74 materials in the simulated humoral environment reached the plateau within \(36\,\mathrm{h}\). It could be considered that under the conditions of simulated body fluids, the micron and nanoscale Mg-MOF74 completely degraded in about 1 day and thus induced a short-term accumulation. In addition, the n-Mg-MOF74 still degraded more rapidly than the m-Mg-MOF74 in a pure simulated body fluid environment. However, the result of this ion release reversed when the living cells existed: The \(\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}\) release profile in the extracellular fluid of the nanoscale group was steadier than the micron-scale group, following with a lower final concentration (Fig. 2(i)).
This opposite outcome reminded us of the importance of a further consideration to the interactions between Mg-MOF74 and cells rather than ignore the internal environment and simply assess the cytotoxicity of the material itself. Following this direction, we traced the occurrence of the story during the material degradation process in the presence of cells marked by, observed as green dots, FITC-fluorescence-labelled m-Mg-MOF74 and n-Mg-MOF74 (Fig. 2(j)). The biodistributions of these green dots revealed both qualitatively and quantitatively evident diversity in nano- and micron-sized groups, with a large number of dots in the former groups localized chiefly intracellularly while few of them scarcely remained outside the cells in the latter ones. This phenomenon was most possibly ascribed to the fact that free extracellular FITC molecules could be eliminated with ease, yet the green fluorescence tended to be preserved when they were incorporated into cells. Specifically, the n-Mg-MOF74 could enter the cell, yet the m-Mg-MOF74 only existed extracellularly due to its size limitation. Rapid increase in ion concentration had
been reported to generate an unbalanced change in osmotic stress that might adversely affect physiological functions of the cell membrane and consequently jeopardize cell survival [44, 46]. Since n-Mg-MOF74 could be taken up intracellularly within a suitable feeding range, the concentrations of \(\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}\) inside and outside the cells were balanced, thereby alleviating the possible harm caused by the excessively high osmotic pressure and finally reducing the cytotoxicity of the material. Similar to other advanced nanoparticle materials, the potential scenarios of n-Mg-MOF74 might be greatly broadened owing to its nano particle size [19, 26, 47].
## 3.3 In vitro biological potential of initial osteogenesis-related assay
Both micron and nanoscale Mg-MOFs could release magnesium ions slowly in body fluids. Magnesium is the essential element in the human body and plays an important role in the process of osteogenic differentiation [32]. In the previous section, the positive results of toxicity assessments motivated us to further explore the biological potential of m/n-Mg-MOF74 and compared the possible differences between them. Firstly, we identified BMSCs by flow cytometry (CytomicsTM FC 500) (Fig. S11 in the ESM), and then determined the safe dose of Mg-MOF74 for BMSCs by MTT (Fig. S12 in the ESM) assay and FITC-phalloidin DAPI staining (Fig. 3(a)), and ensured that the next osteogenic experiment was performed within the safe range \((\leq500\;\upmu\mathrm{g}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1})\). We investigated osteogenic activity of BMSCs cultured with the m/n-Mg-MOF74 by measuring the alkaline phosphatase production, collagen secretion and mineralization of the BMSCs cells using Alp, Alizarin Red and Sirius Red staining, respectively.
As was shown in Fig. 3(b), the Intracellular Alp activity of cells under the treatment of \(100\;\upmu\mathrm{g}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) n-Mg-MOF74 was significantly greater \((p\,<\,0.05)\) than those under any other dose of n-Mg-MOF74 and m-Mg-MOF74 after culturing for 7 days. This trend became more evident in the Alp protein activity assay. Based on the statistical histogram (Fig. 3(c)), the BMSCs had the highest Alp activity in n100 group than any other groups. In addition, the level of Alp expression in gene also determined that the n100 group got the strongest advantage (Fig. 3(f)). Furthermore, collagen production quantified by Sirius Red staining showed that more collagen was secreted under moderate amount of m/n-Mg-MOF74 treatment at the early stage (Day 7). Compared with the MOFs of different particle sizes, apparently denser collagen was deposited in the n-Mg-MOF74 groups than that in corresponding micron groups (Fig. 3(d)). Notably, collagen secretion by cultured cells was highest in n100. These results were further evidenced by collagen 1 (Col1) ELISA analysis and RT-PCR assay (Figs. 3(e) and 3(g)). Based on the optical images and quantitative assay (Fig. S13 in the ESM), the BMSCs displayed the same denser mineral nodules and degree of mineralization on Day 7, except for those of the m/n500 groups that were slightly lower.
To further evaluate the osteogenic activity at the molecular level, we investigated the transcript and expression levels of an important early osteogenic gene, runt-related transcription factor 2 (Runx2) [48], under different doses of m/n-Mg-MOF74 by RT-PCR and Western-bolt. At the early stage of (Day 7), there was much higher Runx2 expression in the BMSCs cultured with n-Mg-MOF74 than that with m-Mg-MOF74 (Fig. 3(h)). Then the n100 exhibited notably higher expression levels of Runx2 compared with any other groups \((p<0.05)\). Moreover, the Western-bolt results of Runx2 expression in n50-200 groups were markedly higher than groups of the same range in the micro or control groups (Fig. 3(i)).
Alp acts as a critical role in a variety of cellular activities, especially in early osteogenic differentiation [49]. On the other hand, collagen I production, sedimentation and mineralization are the cornerstones of bone formation [50]. Our study demonstrated that Mg-MOF74 which continuously delivered \({\mathrm{Mg}}^{2+}\) could up-regulate Runx2, promote Alp synthesis and collagen secretion, revealing excellent osteogenic potential of this type of MOFs. Interestingly, in comparison of these micron and nanoscale materials, n-Mg-MOF74 exhibited
more advanced capability of osteogenic differentiation under the same concentration. This finding might be on account of the fact that the magnesium ion of n-Mg-MOF74 could enter the cell more easily as was mentioned in Fig. 2(j). Upon the influx of \({\mathrm{Mg}}^{2+}\) and the subsequent activation of the osteogenesis-related pathway, the \({\mathrm{Mg}}^{2+}\)-enriched microenvironment remarkably enhanced the proliferation, migration, and osteogenic differentiation of encapsulated BMSCs [32]. In a word, Mg-MOF74 could be applied as a modified material to promote
initial osteogenesis and its nanoscale member had more potency in this field.
## 3.4 In vitro biological potential of angiogenesis assay
In addition to the osteogenesis process, angiogenesis is another common and critical physiological activity in human body. Magnesium ions had been shown to act as a critical role in the angiogenic process that, for instance, they were able to guide vascular localization, growth and even connectivity [32, 33]. Hence it was necessary to verify the angiogenic promoting properties of Mg-MOF74 and explore the possible advantages of n-Mg-MOF74. Identical to osteogenesis research, the suitable therapeutic dose of Mg-MOF74 for HUVECs was determined (Fig. S14 in the ESM and Fig. 4(a)). Cell migration assay was characterized by crystal violet staining of the permeabilized cells. The experimental results showed that all of the Mg-MOF74 groups had significant effects on the migration of HUVEC (Fig. 4(b)) compared with the control group. Further, the promoting effect of n100-200 groups was significantly greater than those of other Mg-MOF74 groups, indicating that n-Mg-MOF74 had a higher upper limit of angiogenic effects over a suitable concentration range. In vitro angiogenesis study of the \(\mathrm{m/n-Mg-MOF74}\) was mainly composed of Matrigel experiment which was implemented to characterize the effect of released components from the materials on the angiogenesis of vascular endothelial cell. We took the culture medium of different concentrations of Mg-MOF groups from which had cultured HUVECs for 3 days to finish Matrigel experiments. It could be discovered that the tube density under medium or low doses groups (m50-100, n50-200) was obviously higher than those of the other groups (Fig. S15 in the ESM). And the following tube statistics by Image J also showed significant differences among them. Meanwhile, the scratch test revealed that the "scar" recovery was the most significant in the m100, n100 and n200 groups after \(24~\mathrm{h}\) in the scratch test, which demonstrated that the benign effects on HUVECs was Mg-MOF74-concentration-dependent within an optimum range of dose (Fig. 4(c)). It was worth noting that the highest promoting effect still came from nano-scale groups rather than the micro-scale groups.
Previous studies had stated that magnesium ions could effectively promote the synthesis and secretion of NO in endothelial cells [33]. Being a small molecule, NO is regulated by important vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF) and is able to activate many key proteins of angiogenesis (such as angiogenin vWF), which allows itself to be a critical node in neovascularization [51]. Therefore, in order to further understand the mechanism of angiogenesis promotion of Mg-MOF74, we investigated the NO exocrine situation after treatment by each group and analyzed it at the molecular level of genes. We could observe in Fig. 4(d) that compared with the untreated group, the levels of NO secreted from the HUVECs of each Mg-MOF74 group significantly increased and, moreover, the trends in n100 and n200 groups appeared to be more prominent. The following characterization of endothelial nitric oxide synthase (eNos) also corresponded to the above trend: the various concentrations of Mg-MOF74 used in the experiments promote the replication and transcription of eNos gene, and the nano-scale Mg-MOF74 had better effect than micro-scale groups (Figs. 4(e) and 4(f)).
The biological function of vascular endothelial cells is closely related to the concentration of available magnesium ions. An appropriate composition of magnesium in materials can not only promote cell adhesion, but also enhance their ability to migrate and activate vascular potential [52, 53]. In
this study, \({\mathrm{Mg}}^{2+}\) was released from \(\mathrm{m/n-Mg-MOF74}\) to promote angiogenesis, so its effect ultimately depended on the amount of \({\mathrm{Mg}}^{2+}\) actually utilized by HUVECs. The very advantage of the nano-groups was contributed to its easy and accessible incorporation by the cells and the following sustainable release. Therefore, the bioavailability of magnesium in the nanoscale groups preformed higher than that of the microparticles under the same input condition. This finding also explained why the expression and secretion levels of NO synthesis-related genes were more regulated by nanoscale Mg-MOF74.
## 3.5 In vivo toxicity evaluation
To validate the in vitro findings, the in vivo biosafety of Mg-MOF74 materials was conducted. The biocompatibility of Mg-MOFs was assessed in rats through intraperitoneal injection administration. First, the injected dose of each group of rats was calculated based on the body weight and total amount of blood of the rats [54], matching with the concentration of the in vivo toxicity experiments. No obvious increase of body weight was found between treated and untreated rat except for the highest dose group of nanomaterials (n2000), and this difference became more significant in m-Mg-MOF74 groups (m2000) (Fig. 5(a)). This finding indicated that the in vivo toxicity of \(\mathrm{m/n-Mg-MOF74}\) were still concentration-dependent, and it would still have a negative impact on animal survival when the amount of injection was too much. The trends of body weight also indicated that the n-Mg-MOF74 had less effect on the growth in contrast with their micron-sized particles. \(\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}\) and \(\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\), two important divalent ions in the body, are closely related in many physiological processes [45, 55]. In Fig. 5(b), we observed that the \({\mathrm{Mg}}^{2+}\) concentration in the blood increasing in groups above \(\mathrm{n}/\mathrm{m}500\) with microscale groups raised more obvious \((\mathrm{n}2000>\mathrm{n}2000,\mathrm{m}1000>\mathrm{n}1000)\), which might also contribute to the cellular uptake demonstrated by in vitro experiments. At the same time, the slight decrease in the concentration of calcium ions in the blood was in synchronization with the increase of the concentration of magnesium ions, and it was most likely due to the fact that the two ion buffer pairs are similar and enter the cell through the same ion channel [56] (Fig. 5(c)). Furthermore, none of the m/n-Mg-MOF74 showed any hematological toxicity, which was identical with the good biocompatibility of \({\mathrm{Mg}}^{2+}\) (Fig. S16 in the ESM).
To further explore the in vivo biocompatibility of Mg-MOF74, some critical protein groups contents in serum with corresponding organs were monitoring. Firstly, according to the guidance of previous toxicity studies [57], we studied the oxidative threat of two Mg-MOF74 and found no significant evidence of toxicity (Fig. S17 in the ESM). Heart is the vital organ in the animal, and the concentration changes of ions in the blood affect the physiological function of the heart. Some researchers had reported that high levels of magnesium in the blood might be equal to hypercalcemia causing acute cardiotoxicity [58, 59]. In this study, we evaluated the potential hazard of Mg-MOF74 to the heart from multiple aspects: cardiac structure, CK and persistent index lactate dehydrogenase levels. In Fig. 5(g) and Fig. S18 in the ESM, the specimen photograph of the heart showed no significant structural change except for the m2000 group being small. However, tissue slices indicated that the myocardial damage were associated with Mg-MOF74 concentration and particle size during the gradual increase in treatment concentration, myocardial fibers showed inflammatory cell infiltration, edema and even dissolved necrosis (m2000). Noteworthy, the same dose of n-Mg-MOF74 myocardial injury was smaller (m1000 vs. n1000, m2000 vs. n2000). In addition, as the structural
image displayed, numerical changes in early myocardial injury index CK and subacute LDH levels also confirmed cardiac structural observations (Figs. 5(e) and 5(f)). Next, MOFs were often reported to be mainly metabolized by the kidneys as decomposition products or prototype [19]. We analyzed the
urea and creatinine levels of SD rats after Mg-MOF74 treatment. According to the BUN shown in Fig. 5(d), only the high concentration groups \((\mathrm{n}/\mathrm{m}1000,2000)\) caused a transient increase at Day 1, and all BUN level returned to normal level with the images of kidney organs in each group of rats (Fig. S19 in
the ESM) displaying no damage at Day 7. For other important organs such as lung, liver and spleen, these H&E staining results (Fig. S19 in the ESM) showed no significant toxicity or damage.
Additionally, the weight of those rats in \(\mathrm{n}/\mathrm{m}2000\) groups gradually increased and approached the weight levels of other groups as time went by and no abnormality was found in the BUN, LDH-L, calcium, magnesium, CK and ALT detections at Day 21. So \(\mathrm{n/m-Mg-MOF74}\) did not show significant long-term persistent toxicity. In the analysis of the animal toxicity model, both \(\mathrm{m/n-Mg-MOF74}\) showed excellent biosafety on account of the excellent biocompatibility of magnesium and high in vivo clearance efficiency (Fig. S20 in the ESM), with limited myocardial toxicity occurring only at very high doses. It was worth mentioning that \(\mathrm{n-Mg-MOF74}\) appeared less toxic than the microscale group, which was consistent with the results of our previous in vitro experiments and provided an important basis for its biological applications.
## 3.6 Capacity of \(\mathbf{n}/\mathbf{m}\) -Mg-MOF74 for bone regeneration and angiogenesis in vivo
The regeneration of bone and the reconstruction of blood supply are inseparable [60]. To directly and thoroughly compare the biological potential of different Mg-MOF74, we used \(\mathrm{n/m-Mg-MOF74}\) mixed with bone powder to treat the calvarial defect in rats and focused on the angiogenesis-osteogenesis effect in the surgical site. Micro-CT, H&E staining, Masson's Trichrome staining, Sirius red staining and fluorochrome labeling analysis were performed to assess the recovery of new blood vessels and bones.
After 4 weeks, reconstructed bones distributed around the edge of the defects were found in the \(\mathrm{n-Mg-MOF74}\) and \(\mathrm{m-Mg-MOF74}\) hydrogel groups by 3D images while less newly formed bones could be discovered in the control group (Fig. 6(a)). In addition, most of the bone powder in the \(\mathrm{n/m-Mg-MOF74}\) groups was involved in the formation of new bone; however,
obvious scattered bone powder was found in the reconstruction site of the control group. On the other hand, according to the quantitative micro-CT analysis, the bone volume/tissue volume (BV/TV) and trabecular number of the regenerated bone were compared. The BV/TV in the n-Mg-MOF74 group \((22.06\pm2.83\;\mathrm{mm}^{3})\) was higher than that in the m-Mg-MOF74 group \((17.03\pm1.127\;\mathrm{mm}^{3})\) at 4 weeks, although the BV/TV in both n-Mg-MOF74 and m-Mg-MOF74 were greater than that in the control group (Fig. 6(e)). In Fig. 6(f), the trabecular number was similar in all groups at 4 weeks in which n-Mg-MOF74 achieved the best osteogenesis effect. The results of these 3D osteogenic correlation analyses were consistent with the trends of in vitro experiments.
Histological analysis was performed to assess the outcomes
of calvarial defect recovery. After 4 weeks, H&E staining showed more newly formed bones in the m-Mg-MOF74 and n-Mg-MOF74 groups than those in the control group (Figs. 6(b) and 6(c)). In the n-Mg-MOF74 hydrogel group, nearly all of the bone powder was transformed into confluent bone, suggesting a better osteogenic potential. In addition, the Masson's Trichrome staining and Sirius red staining also proved that both n-Mg-MOF74 and m-Mg-MOF74 could improve collagen deposition and new bone regeneration in the defect areas (Fig. 6(d) and Fig. S20 in the ESM). Alp fluorescent staining, indicating early bone formation activity, was more pronounced in the n/m-Mg-MOF74 groups at 4 weeks after surgery (Figs. 6(e) and 6(g)), suggesting the Mg-MOF74 promoted osteogenesis during the initial period.
Significantly, in addition to regeneration in bone repair, differences in angiogenic effects in the defect areas between different groups could also be observed. As was shown in Fig. 6(c), more pronounced nourishing blood vessels were observed in the newly formed bones near the defect in the \(\mathrm{n}/\mathrm{m}\) -MOF74 groups, which rightly indicated a better reconstruction of blood supply. This finding was more remarkable in vascular endothelial cell-specific CD31 fluorescence images and statistics where the n-Mg-MOF74 group exhibited the most conspicuous fluorescence localization followed with m-MgMOF74 groups. However, the vascular fluorescence of the control group was the poorest (Figs. 6(e) and \(6(\mathrm{g})\) ).
It is critical to ensure the regeneration and migration of vessels during the process of bone recovery because of the nutrients and growth factors delivered from blood [60, 61]. Vascularized bone regeneration [62, 63] provides an appropriate chance for the application of Mg-MOF74, which showed both osteogenesis and angiogenic potential in in vitro experiment. Therefore, by assessing the level of trauma recovery in each rat group, it was possible to comprehensively observe the early osteogenic and angiogenic potential of Mg-MOF74. In this study, benefited from richer Alp and collagen productions, the quantity of newly formed bones in the rats treated with \(\mathrm{n}/\mathrm{m}\) -Mg-MOF74 was superior to those of the control group. Although the differences in levels of Alp and collagen were not significant compared with the m-Mg-MOF74 group, the n-Mg-MOF74 group eventually achieved higher bone formation. This advantage came from the fact that n-Mg-MOF74, a nanoparticle that acted as a magnesium ion delivery-system into the cells, provided a more efficient angiogenesis to accelerate bone reconstruction [32, 64]. In summary, the in vivo usage of both \(\mathrm{n}/\mathrm{m}\) -Mg-MOF74 under appropriate concentrations had the dual osteogenesis angiogenesis biological function, and the nano-scale Mg-MOF74 proved to be a more promising candidate than the micro-scale Mg-MOF74.
## 4 Conclusion
In summary, we evaluated the in vitro and in vivo biosafety of Mg-MOF74 and its nano-sized modified particles followed with assessment of early osteogenesis and angiogenesis promotion. With particle size reduction, n-Mg-MOF74 could be phagocytosed by the cells, leading a balanced increase of ion concentrations both intracellularly and extracellularly. Based on this release model, n-Mg-MOF74 showed lower in vitro cytotoxicity and in vivo cardiotoxicity compared with the micron-scale treatment groups. In addition, n-Mg-MOF74 was also outstanding in the angiogenesis simulation experiment, in which vascular endothelial cells treated under the optimal n-Mg-MOF74 concentration showed the strongest migration activity. More essentially, due to the increased bioavailability of magnesium ions, we observed that n-Mg-MOF74 had a more reliable osteogenic promoting function in the early osteogenic stage. Furthermore, compared with other groups, in vivo vascularized-osteogenesis model also showed the best tissue reconstruction effect under the suitable amount of n-Mg-MOF74 \((100~\upmu\mathrm{g}\,\mathrm{mL}^{-1})\) . This study might present an inspiration to the improvement of biological performance of MOFs or other particulate materials.
